-- The target language of the extractor: a small Java fragment, defined as an
-- abstract syntax tree in Arend.
--
-- WHY THIS EXISTS. `ToJava.ard` used to build Java *source text* directly
-- (`String` concatenation), so the target language existed only implicitly, in
-- the shape of those string literals. This file makes it explicit:
--
--   * it IS the definition of the Java subset we commit to generating -- the
--     generator can only produce what these constructors allow;
--   * it is the object a future operational semantics (and hence any
--     correctness statement about the backend) will be given for;
--   * it separates "which Java construct" from "how it is spelled": printing is
--     one trusted function, `JavaPrint.ard`'s `printClass`, and the generator
--     no longer worries about parentheses, semicolons or whitespace.
--
-- SCOPE. Deliberately exactly what `compileExpr`/`compileDecl` emit, no
-- more: no arithmetic operators, no loops, no conditionals, no visibility
-- modifiers besides `public static`, no generics, no interfaces, no `void`, no
-- array types and no entry point. Classes come in exactly three shapes: the
-- generated program class, the anonymous one-method classes that are our
-- closures (`jClosure`), and the local classes that tie a `fix` knot
-- (`jLocalClass`). Generated code is untyped-style: every value has static type
-- `Object` and is cast where needed, so this fragment is NOT Featherweight Java
-- and is not meant to be.
-- The fixed runtime is hand-written (`runtime/Rt.java`) and never DECLARED
-- here, but it is not all referenced the same way. Its semantic core -- the
-- function type, the data representation, the erased value, and the operations
-- on them -- has dedicated nodes (`tFn`, `tData`, `jApply`, `jClosure`,
-- `jDataNew`, `jDataTag`, `jDataField`, `jBox`; see RUNTIME ABI below), because
-- those are what a correctness statement quantifies over. Its periphery --
-- `Rt.PRIM_*`, `Rt.unbound`, the entry point -- is referenced by NAME
-- (`jStatic`/`jField`/`jCallStatic`), because those are axiom realizations and
-- I/O, opaque to a λ□ semantics and rightly outside this AST.
--
-- COMPARE (rationale and the longer survey live in notes/RESEARCH_AND_PLAN.md):
--   * Malfunction.v (metarocq-malfunction) -- the verified λ□ backend; its
--     `Ident.t := string` / `Longident.t := string` split is `JIdent`/`JQIdent`
--     below, and its `Mvar`/`Mglobal` split is `jVar`/`jStatic`.
--   * CompCert `cfrontend/Clight.v` -- the same two-sort `expr`/`statement`
--     shape (`Ecast`, `Efield`, `Sassign`, `Sswitch`, `Sreturn`), and the same
--     "target AST in the proof assistant, printer outside it" division that
--     CertiCoq's C backend uses (`printClight.ml`).
--   * Featherweight Java (Igarashi-Pierce-Wadler) -- `x | e.f | e.m(e*) |
--     new C(e*) | (C)e` is exactly the expression core here; our extra nodes
--     (literals, assignment, statements) are the ones a code generator cannot
--     avoid.
--   * Jinja / µJava (Isabelle) -- the existing Java-source semantics, and the
--     dissenting design: Jinja folds statements into expressions, we do not.
--
-- IDENTIFIERS. Two type synonyms, both `String`: `JIdent` for a simple Java
-- identifier and `JQIdent` for a possibly dotted (qualified) one. Every such
-- position holds an ALREADY-VALID Java name: mangling happens at exactly one
-- boundary (`mangleKername` in `ToJava.ard`), and generator-invented binders
-- (`p<path>`, `l<path>`, ...) are legal by construction. Plain strings are what
-- every comparable *source-level* backend uses (MetaRocq `ident := string`,
-- lean-to-lambdabox `abbrev Ident := String`, peregrine's sanitizers
-- `utf8_string -> string`); interned integers (CompCert `ident := positive`,
-- CertiCoq λANF `var`) buy cheap maps and provable freshness, neither of which
-- this generator needs.
--
-- STATEMENTS AND EXPRESSIONS ARE TWO SORTS, mutually recursive (a statement
-- contains expressions; an anonymous-class expression contains statements).
-- That mirrors the generator's `Out = \Sigma String String`, a statement
-- prelude plus a value expression; after the retarget it becomes
-- `\Sigma (Array JStmt) JExpr`.
--
-- BINDERS ARE NAMES, not de Bruijn indices: the `path`-derived names are what
-- makes the generator's recursion pass Arend's termination checker without
-- state threading. Well-scopedness can be added later (closure conversion is
-- the natural point).
--
-- COMMENTS are trivia, and they are available in *every* position that
-- occupies a line of its own -- statements (`jComment`) and class members
-- (`jMemberComment`) -- rather than only where the generator happens to need
-- one today. Expressions and types carry no comment node: they have no line to
-- attach one to, and comment-as-expression would have to survive printing in
-- the middle of a term. Nothing else in the AST is annotatable, so this is
-- "everywhere possible".

\import Data.Bool
\import Data.Maybe
\import Data.String

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Identifiers
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- A simple Java identifier: a local, a parameter, a field or a method name.
\func JIdent => String

-- A possibly dotted Java name: `Rt`, `Rt.Fn`, `System.out`,
-- `java.math.BigInteger`, `Long.valueOf`.
\func JQIdent => String

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The runtime class
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Name of the hand-written runtime class (`runtime/Rt.java`). It appears here,
-- next to the AST, rather than in the generator, because the RUNTIME ABI NODES
-- below (`tFn`, `tData`, `jApply`, `jBox`, ...) carry no name of their own: they
-- mean the runtime's function type, data representation and unit, and this is
-- the one place that says how those are spelled. Both the printer and the
-- generator's axiom table read it, so the name is stated once.
--
-- It is a constant, not a generator option. It was briefly a field of a
-- `JavaTarget` record, set to `Rt` by every instance of it -- and unlike the int
-- representation it is not a choice about how λ□ is REPRESENTED, it is the
-- identity of the fixed semantic substrate every generated program shares. Making it
-- configurable would also mean the trusted printer could no longer print a
-- runtime type without being told which runtime, i.e. it would have to take a
-- generator option as a parameter.
\func runtimeClassName : JIdent => "Rt"

-- `Rt.` -- the qualifier of every reference to a runtime member.
\func runtimeQual : String => runtimeClassName ++ "."

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Types
--
-- Only what appears in generated code: `Object` (everything -- every value has
-- static type `Object`), the two runtime types, and a named class
-- (`RuntimeException`, a `fix` local class).
--
-- There is no `void` and no array type: both existed only for the generated
-- entry point (`void main(String[] args)`), which is no longer generated -- a
-- program's `main` is now written by hand outside the extractor, so the fragment
-- has no statement that is not part of computing a value.
--
-- `tFn` and `tData` are SEPARATE from `tClass`, even though they print as
-- `Rt.Fn` and `Rt.Data`: they are the two types the runtime ABI is made of, and
-- a semantics has to give them meaning (a function space, a tagged product)
-- rather than treat them as opaque named classes. As `tClass "Rt.Fn"` they were
-- indistinguishable from any user class that happened to bear that name, so
-- every rule about application or constructors would have had to match on a
-- string literal. See the RUNTIME ABI section under `JExpr`.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\data JType
  | tObject
  -- The runtime's one-method function interface: λ□'s function space.
  | tFn
  -- The runtime's constructor representation: a tag plus a field array.
  | tData
  | tClass JQIdent

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Expressions
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\data JExpr
  -- A local variable or method parameter used as a value: `p3`, `l7`.
  -- (Malfunction's `Mvar`, Clight's `Evar`, Featherweight Java's `x`.)
  | jVar JIdent
  -- A qualified name used as a value or as the receiver of a static member:
  -- `Rt`, `System.out`. Kept apart from `jVar` because it is resolved
  -- globally, not in the local environment (Malfunction's `Mglobal`).
  | jStatic JQIdent
  -- `e.f` -- field access: `d.tag`, `d.fields`, `Rt.BOX`, `Rt.PRIM_ADD_INT`.
  | jField JExpr JIdent
  -- `(T)(e)` -- a downcast; unavoidable, since every value is typed `Object`.
  | jCast JType JExpr
  -- `e.m(args)` -- instance method call: `.apply(a)`, `System.out.println(..)`.
  | jInvoke JExpr JIdent (Array JExpr)
  -- `m(args)` where `m` may be qualified: a generated constant `c_foo()`,
  -- `Rt.unbound(i, path)`, `Long.valueOf(5L)`.
  | jCallStatic JQIdent (Array JExpr)
  -- `new T(args)` -- `new RuntimeException("no matching branch")`, and the
  -- instantiation of a `fix` local class. (A `Rt.Data` is `jDataNew` instead.)
  | jNew JType (Array JExpr)
  -- --- RUNTIME ABI ---------------------------------------------------------
  --
  -- The five nodes below are the ones a λ□ correctness statement is actually
  -- about: they are λ□'s `box`, `lambda`, `app`, `construct` and the field/tag
  -- reads that `case` and `proj` perform. Each USED to be a combination of the
  -- general Java nodes above over hard-coded member-name strings --
  -- `jInvoke (jCast (tClass "Rt.Fn") f) "apply" [x]`,
  -- `d.fields[3]` built from a generic array-index node -- which had two costs:
  --
  --   * the AST could not distinguish the runtime's application from any other
  --     call of a method spelled `apply`, so a semantics (and any optimisation
  --     or scope check) would have to recognise the ABI by matching string
  --     literals, and would be silently wrong for a program that used those
  --     names for something else;
  --   * the invariants were unstated. `jApply` cannot be given a non-`Fn`
  --     cast, `jDataField` cannot be given a non-`Nat` index, and a closure is
  --     necessarily over `Fn` -- all of which were previously just facts about
  --     which expressions the generator happened to build.
  --
  -- They are still only ABI, not semantics: what `apply` DOES is fixed by
  -- `runtime/Rt.java`, and a future semantics assumes it rather than deriving it
  -- from that file. The point is that it now has a syntactic node to assume it
  -- OF. How each one is spelled is `JavaPrint.ard`'s business, and every one of
  -- them prints exactly the text its string-based predecessor did.

  -- `Rt.BOX` -- the value of an erased proof or type (λ□ `box`). A single shared
  -- object, so this is a constant and not an allocation.
  | jBox
  -- `((Rt.Fn)(f)).apply(x)` -- λ□ `app`. The cast is part of the node because
  -- every value has static type `Object`: it is not an optional annotation but
  -- how application is spelled in this fragment.
  | jApply JExpr JExpr
  -- `new Rt.Fn(){ public Object apply(Object <param>){ <body> } }` -- λ□
  -- `lambda`, as an anonymous class (no `invokedynamic`).
  -- The method is not a `JMethod` but spelled out as (parameter name, body): it
  -- is always `public Object apply(Object)`, and a record field would put
  -- `JMethod` into the JExpr/JStmt recursion, which Arend rejects (records
  -- cannot be part of a mutual `\data` cycle).
  -- Previously `jAnonFn JType ..`, parametrised by the interface -- but `tFn`
  -- was the only value ever passed, so the parameter existed solely to give a
  -- semantics a case it had to rule out.
  -- Temporary by design: closure conversion (step 5) replaces it with named
  -- `Closure_<path>` classes.
  | jClosure JIdent (Array JStmt)
  -- `new Rt.Data(tag, new Object[]{ fields })` -- λ□ `construct`. The tag is a
  -- `Nat` because it is a constructor index, and the field array is built here
  -- rather than by a general array-literal node, which is why no such node
  -- exists any more.
  | jDataNew Nat (Array JExpr)
  -- `d.tag` -- the constructor index of a data value; the scrutinee of the
  -- `switch` a λ□ `case` becomes.
  | jDataTag JExpr
  -- `d.fields[i]` -- the i-th field. `Nat`, not `JExpr`: every index the
  -- generator produces is a literal position, and λ□ offers no computed field
  -- access. This is also where `proj`'s `npars + idx` is added (on the Arend
  -- side), which is why the fragment needs no arithmetic node.
  | jDataField JExpr Nat

  -- --- end RUNTIME ABI -----------------------------------------------------

  -- `C.this` -- the enclosing instance of the local class named `C`, as seen
  -- from inside a nested anonymous class. Needed by the local-class encoding of
  -- `fix` (`ToJava.ard`): a sibling of a mutually recursive block is referenced
  -- from within a `jClosure` whose own `this` is the closure, so the forwarding
  -- call has to name the instance it means.
  -- NOT `jStatic "C.this"`, even though that would print identically: `jStatic`
  -- means a globally resolved name, and this one is resolved in the lexical
  -- scope of the enclosing class. The distinction is the point of this AST.
  | jQualThis JQIdent
  -- A `String` literal; the printer is responsible for quoting and escaping.
  | jStringLit String
  -- A decimal `int` literal: constructor tags, field indices, array indices,
  -- array sizes. Nat, because every such number is a position or a count.
  | jIntLit Nat
  -- A `long` literal (`5L`), i.e. `Int` because it may be negative. Only used
  -- to build λ□ `prim` int values (`Long.valueOf(5L)`), which is the only
  -- representation the generator emits (see KNOWN MISMATCH in ToJava.ard).
  | jLongLit Int

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Statements
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\data JStmt
  -- A local variable declaration, optionally `final` and optionally
  -- initialized: `final Object l3 = e;`, `Object r3;`.
  -- An uninitialized one is exactly the `case` result variable, assigned in
  -- every branch.
  | jLocal (isFinal : Bool) JType JIdent (Maybe JExpr)
  -- `lhs = rhs;` where `lhs` is a name or an array element (the `fix` knot).
  | jAssign JExpr JExpr
  -- `switch (scrut) { cases default: dflt }`, where a case is
  -- `case <tag>: { <stmts> break; }` -- the printer emits the block and the
  -- `break`, so this fragment has no fallthrough. The cases are a `\Sigma`
  -- rather than a record for the same reason as `jClosure` above (and as
  -- `LambdaBox.ard`'s `case` branches); Malfunction's
  -- `Mswitch of t * (case list * t) list` makes the same choice.
  | jSwitch JExpr (Array (\Sigma Nat (Array JStmt))) (Array JStmt)
  -- `class <C> { public Object <m>(Object <p>){ <body> } ... }` -- a LOCAL class
  -- (declared inside a block, capturing the enclosing effectively-final locals),
  -- holding k methods that may call each other. This is the second encoding of
  -- λ□ `fix`: the recursive knot is tied by the class declaration itself, so
  -- there are no mutable cells and no initialization order to reason about.
  -- Like `jClosure`, a method is spelled out as (name, parameter, body) rather
  -- than held as a `JMethod`: every one of them is `public Object m(Object)`,
  -- and a record field would put `JMethod` into the JExpr/JStmt `\data` cycle,
  -- which Arend rejects.
  | jLocalClass JIdent (Array (\Sigma JIdent JIdent (Array JStmt)))
  -- `throw e;`
  | jThrow JExpr
  -- `return e;`
  | jReturn JExpr
  -- `// <text>` on a line of its own; see COMMENTS above.
  | jComment String


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Members and the program class
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- A method of the generated class: a λ□ constant (`c_foo`) or the program's
-- entry value (`body`). (The `apply` of a closure is part of `jClosure`, and a
-- `fix` sibling part of `jLocalClass`, so neither is a `JMethod`.)
--
-- Only a NAME and a BODY. Every method the generator emits is
-- `public static Object <name>()`: static because the class is a namespace and
-- never instantiated, `Object` because that is the only type a λ□ value has, and
-- zero-argument because λ□ constants take no arguments -- a function is a
-- closure VALUE, not a parameterized method. This record used to carry
-- `mIsStatic`, `mReturn` and `mParams` as well; all three had a single possible
-- value once the generated `main` -- the one `void` method, the one with a
-- parameter, and the only reason `tVoid`, `tArray` and `JParam` existed -- went
-- away.
\record JMethod
  (mName : JIdent)
  (mBody : Array JStmt)

\data JMember
  | jMethod JMethod
  -- `// <text>` in class-body position: what an erased inductive produces.
  | jMemberComment String

-- One generated class: `public final class <className> { ... }`.
--
-- The NAME IS DATA, not the constant `Prog` it used to be, because two λ□
-- programs extracted separately have to be able to coexist -- and, later, to
-- call each other. It is an argument of `compileClass`/`compileProgram` rather
-- than a generator option: it says what to call the output, not how to compile
-- it.
--
-- The class has no entry point. It is a LIBRARY: a `body()` returning the
-- program's value, plus one method per λ□ constant. Whoever wants to run it
-- writes a `main` that calls `body()` (see `runtime/Main.java.in`), which is
-- what makes the output reusable and combinable instead of a program that can
-- only print itself.
\record JClass (className : JIdent) (classMembers : Array JMember)
